g=A.ce(A.a7(["Python",'# Initialize an array (list) of size 5 with zeros\nnumbers = [0] * 5\n# Assign a value to the first index\nnumbers[0] = 42\n# Access and print the value\nprint(f"First item: {numbers[0]}")\n',"Java",'// Declare and initialize an array of size 5\nint[] numbers = new int[5];\n// Assign a value to the first index\nnumbers[0] = 42;\n// Access and print the value\nSystem.out.println("First item: " + numbers[0]);\n',"C++",'#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int numbers[5];\n    numbers[0] = 42;\n    cout << "First item: " << numbers[0] << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n',"JavaScript","// Initialize an array\nconst numbers = new Array(5).fill(0);\n// Assign a value\nnumbers[0] = 42;\n// Access and print\nconsole.log(`First item: ${numbers[0]}`);\n"],d,d),e,f,"Arrays are the most fundamental data structure in computer science. They store elements in contiguous memory locations, enabling direct access by index in O(1) time. Understanding arrays is the foundation for nearly every algorithm and data structure you will encounter.","array-intro",m,"Array Introduction",A.a(["Out-of-bounds access causes runtime errors or undefined behaviour","Fixed size means overflow is silent in low-level languages like C","Confusing array index (starts at 0) with position (starts at 1)"],h),g,A.a([A.a0(1,"An array is a linear data structure that collects elements of the exact same data type and stores them right next to each other in memory.",A.a(["A dynamic data structure that grows infinitely.","A collection of elements of the same data type stored at contiguous memory locations.","A collection of elements of different data types.","A mathematical function that returns multiple values."],h),"What is an array in computer science?"),A.a0(2,"Most standard programming languages use zero-based indexing, meaning the computer starts counting at 0. The first element is always at index 0.",A.a(["1","-1","0","It depends on the array size"],h),"In most programming languages (like Java, C++, Dart), what is the index of the first element in an array?"),A.a0(0,"Arrays store elements in contiguous memory. The computer can instantly calculate the exact memory address using math, making access O(1) lightning fast!",A.a(["O(1)","O(n)","O(log n)","O(n\xb2)"],h),"What is the time complexity of accessing an element in an array if you already know its index?"),A.a0(1,"Linear Search works exactly how it sounds: it iterates through the array one by one in a straight line until it finds the matching element or reaches the end.",A.a(["It splits the array in half repeatedly.","It checks each element sequentially from start to finish.","It sorts the array first and then searches.","It uses a hash function to find the exact index."],h),l),A.a0(2,"The best-case scenario is when the target element happens to be the very first item in the array. It is found immediately on the first try, taking O(1) time.",A.a(["O(n)","O(log n)","O(1)","O(n\xb2)"],h),k),A.a0(1,"In the worst case, the item you are looking for is at the very end of the array (or not in it at all). You have to check every single element, which takes O(n) time.",A.a(["O(1)","O(n)","O(log n)","O(n log n)"],h),j),A.a0(2,"Because arrays are 0-indexed, an array of size 5 only has valid indexes from 0 to 4. Accessing index 5 looks past the allocated memory and crashes the program.",A.a(["It returns 0.","It returns null.","It throws an Index Out Of Bounds error.","It resizes the array automatically."],h),"What happens if you try to access array[5] in an array that only has 5 elements?"),A.a0(1,"Standard static arrays are fixed in size. Once created in memory, they cannot grow or shrink. If you need a resizable array, you must use a Dynamic Array (like an ArrayList).",A.a(["They can only store text strings.","They have a fixed size that cannot change once created.","They are very slow to read data from.","They use node pointers to link memory blocks together."],h),"Which of the following is a major limitation of a standard static array?"),A.a0(1,"To insert at the beginning (index 0), every single existing element must be physically copied and shifted one space to the right to make room, taking O(n) time.",A.a(["Because memory needs to be completely re-allocated.","Because all existing elements must be shifted over by one position.","Because the computer has to search for the element first.","It is actually a fast O(1) operation."],h),"Why is inserting a new element at the very beginning of an array considered a slow O(n) operation?"),A.a0(2,"Linear Search is perfectly fine for small, unsorted arrays because of its simple logic. For large, sorted arrays, an algorithm like Binary Search is much more efficient.",A.a(["When the array is massive (millions of items).","When the array is already sorted.","When dealing with a small, unsorted array.",i],h),"When is Linear Search the best algorithm to choose?")],c),"assets/mds/dsa/array/array_intro.md",B.aa5)
