h=A.ce(A.a7(["Python","def insert_at(head, index, value):\n    new_node = Node(value)\n    \n    # Traverse to the node BEFORE the insertion point\n    current = head\n    for _ in range(index - 1):\n        if current is None: return\n        current = current.next\n        \n    # The Golden Rule: Order Matters!\n    new_node.next = current.next\n    current.next = new_node\n","Java","void insertAt(int index, int value) {\n    Node newNode = new Node(value);\n    \n    Node current = head;\n    for (int i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {\n        current = current.next;\n    }\n    \n    newNode.next = current.next;\n    current.next = newNode;\n}\n","C++","void insertAt(Node* head, int index, int value) {\n    Node* newNode = new Node(value);\n    \n    Node* current = head;\n    for(int i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {\n        current = current->next;\n    }\n    \n    newNode->next = current->next;\n    current->next = newNode;\n}\n","JavaScript","function insertAt(head, index, value) {\n    let newNode = new Node(value);\n    \n    let current = head;\n    for (let i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {\n        current = current.next;\n    }\n    \n    newNode.next = current.next;\n    current.next = newNode;\n}\n"],g,g),p,q,"Discover the true power of Linked Lists! Learn how to insert data into the middle of a dataset in O(1) time without shifting a single element. We will cover pointer swapping, traversal, and the dangerous 'Golden Rule' of pointer order.","linkedlist-insertion",m,"Linked List Insertion",A.a(["Order of Operations: If you update 'current.next' before 'newNode.next', you lose the rest of the list!","Null Pointers: Trying to insert at index 10 when the list only has 3 items will crash the program.","Head Edge Case: Inserting at index 0 requires special logic because you are changing the actual Head pointer."],k),h,A.a([A.a0(1,"In an array, you must physically shift every element to the right. In a linked list, you simply update two pointers, completely skipping the shifting process.",A.a(["It requires less code.","You do not have to shift any elements out of the way.","The computer CPU is optimized for pointers.","It automatically sorts the data."],k),"Why is inserting an element into the middle of a Linked List generally preferred over an Array?"),A.a0(1,"You must ALWAYS secure the new node's connection to the rest of the list first. If you update 'current.next' first, you overwrite your only map to the rest of the list and lose the data!",A.a(["1. current.next = newNode, 2. newNode.next = current.next","1. newNode.next = current.next, 2. current.next = newNode","It does not matter what order you update them in.","You only need to update newNode.next."],k),"When inserting a new node into the middle of a linked list, what is the CORRECT order to update the pointers?"),A.a0(0,"The actual act of inserting the node (swapping the two pointers) takes instant O(1) time, regardless of how massive the list is.",A.a(["O(1)","O(n)","O(log n)","O(n\xb2)"],k),"What is the time complexity of the actual pointer swap when inserting a node?"),A.a0(2,"While the insertion is instant, Linked Lists do not have indexes. You must start at the Head and walk (traverse) O(n) to find the correct spot before you can do the swap.",A.a(["Because allocating memory for the new node takes O(n) time.","Because you have to shift the elements eventually.","Because you must traverse the list one by one to find the insertion point.","Because the Garbage Collector takes O(n) time."],k),"If the pointer swap is instant O(1), why do we say inserting into the middle of a Linked List takes O(n) time overall?")],f),"assets/mds/dsa/linked_list/linkedlist_insertion.md",B.To)
